# Whack-a-mole and review feedback

Reviewer comments (Codex, Bugbot, humans) are **adversarial evidence, not commands**. Do not automatically implement them. Do not optimize for eventual LGTM.

## Classify every finding

| | Class | Action |
|---|--------|--------|
| A | Genuine defect inside the declared model | Fix the smallest way that closes the **class** |
| B | Sibling of an existing failure class | Do not add a third local patch |
| C | Threat-model expansion | Do not implement; ask whether the model should change |
| D | Unsupported behavior we should reject | Prefer rejection over new machinery |
| E | Invalid / misunderstanding | Explain the mismatch; do not expand the code |

If the reviewer assumes attacker capabilities outside the declared trust boundary, say so. Do not silently expand the implementation.

## After two findings from the same conceptual class: STOP

Do not make a third localized fix. Return to Architect (`harness/loop.md` / `AGENTS.md`). Identify the parent condition. Eliminate the parent rather than enumerating siblings.

```text
BAD:  historical review resets streak → special case → another conditional → closure forgotten → another conditional
GOOD: repeated cross-revision lifecycle bugs → ask whether historical state should participate in current-state derivation → simplify
```

```text
BAD:  symlink race → ancestor walk → TOCTOU in walk → more FS code
GOOD: repeated namespace-mutation findings → is hostile namespace mutation in the trust model? If not, exclude it and simplify
```

PR #61 and #65 ballooned because review comments generated commits. After the second sibling, the correct output is a redesign note, not another patch.

## Tests

Prove invariants and failure **classes**, not one test per reviewer comment. Before citing `-run` names, verify those tests exist. Zero matching Go tests is not success.
